OK. I should add that I happily use at(1) to do something later ...
although less nowadays when (like you) I use screen so I'll always
have a working shell (sleep 1h && do_something, and so on).

I suppose I could use batch(1) on the multiuser systems at work ...
but it would feel a bit unpredictable since the load never drops much
overnight, and atd's default loadavg limit of 1.5 is probably the
wrong one since we have dozens of CPUs ...  Also, they have managed to
break local delivery of mail so I wouldn't be notified :-P

More generally, I have a feeling load average is no longer the best
measure of free resources.  Disk and network I/O can easily be the
bottleneck, with today's absurdly fast CPUs.

